\begin{abstract}
\noindent We present the final deployment configuration and structural physics expansion for the Sovereign Plasmonic Energy Harvesting Simulator (SPEHS-v1.0). This iteration introduces a quantum-cryptographic framework that maps real-time celestial Stokes polarization vectors ($\mathbf{S}$) directly onto two-state density matrices ($\bm{\rho}_{\text{qubit}}$), establishing an unhackable hardware phase interlock within the carrier extraction system. Concurrently, macro-surface implementation risks are evaluated via a generalized polar Kirsch solution to map cyclic thermo-mechanical and Maxwell radiation pressure loading fields around a periodic $r_0 = 3.5\,\mu\text{m}$ nanorod array. Multi-physics control loop execution verifies a peak boundary von Mises stress concentration of $2435.54\text{ MPa}$ and a spatial mean matrix load of $1058.08\text{ MPa}$. Results demonstrate that inward Maxwell compressive clamping vectors successfully stabilize sub-wavelength noble and transition metal lattices against extreme non-equilibrium thermal expansion fields, maintaining structural integrity across continuous, windowless structural boundaries.
\end{abstract}

