\section{Interfacial Boundary Elastic Mechanics}
\subsection{Kirsch Stress Formulations}
The inclusion of a periodic array of $r_0 = 3.5\,\mu\text{m}$ sub-wavelength nanorods within an anisotropic semiconductor matrix creates intense mechanical load points. Under extreme thermal expansion stresses ($\sigma_{\text{thermal}} = 966.3716\text{ MPa}$ derived via the Navier-Cauchy framework), the localized stress tensor field as a function of radial coordinates $(R, \theta)$ is modeled via an adapted plane-strain Kirsch solution:
\begin{align}
\sigma_{rr} &= \frac{\sigma_{\text{th}}}{2} \left(1 - \frac{r_0^2}{R^2}\right) + \frac{\sigma_{\text{th}}}{2} \left(1 + \frac{3r_0^4}{R^4} - \frac{4r_0^2}{R^2}\right)\cos(2\theta) \\
\sigma_{\theta\theta} &= \frac{\sigma_{\text{th}}}{2} \left(1 + \frac{r_0^2}{R^2}\right) - \frac{\sigma_{\text{th}}}{2} \left(1 + \frac{3r_0^4}{R^4}\right)\cos(2\theta) \\
\tau_{r\theta} &= -\frac{\sigma_{\text{th}}}{2} \left(1 - \frac{3r_0^4}{R^4} + \frac{2r_0^2}{R^2}\right)\sin(2\theta)
\end{align}
Stresses are converted to Cartesian tensor elements ($\sigma_{xx}, \sigma_{yy}, \tau_{xy}$) across a high-resolution spatial grid using standard transformation matrices to map directional shear fields across the host material boundaries.

\subsection{von Mises Yield Verification}
To evaluate localized material yield and potential crystal cleavage failure paths at the nanorod-matrix junction, the complete Cartesian strain matrix is evaluated using the yield metric:
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{vM} = \sqrt{\sigma_{xx}^2 - \sigma_{xx}\sigma_{yy} + \sigma_{yy}^2 + 3\tau_{xy}^2}
\end{equation}
Numerical tracking of the spatial matrix boundaries ($R \ge r_0$) outputs a peak localized boundary stress of \textbf{$2435.54\text{ MPa}$}, settling into an integrated grid average of \textbf{$1058.08\text{ MPa}$}. 

Crucially, the inward compressive force vector derived from the Maxwell radiation pressure tensor ($P_{\text{rad}} \approx 4.9664\text{ Pa}$) applies a localized electromagnetic clamp over the mesh boundary. This pressure vector counteracts the peak outward shear components, preserving the elastic lattice parameters and allowing the continuous metamaterial skin to resist cyclic failure under severe thermal gradients without requiring mechanical stress-relief gaps.

